## UX notes — silkweb-1.51 Preview typography polish
Foundation: `Resources/preview.css` (body 16px/1.6, `.sw-doc` 720px column), 1.47 `SilkwebEditorHeading`, 1.50 tab bar. These values are original Silkweb choices. Only heading and top-spacing rules change; body, code, tables, lists, TOC and images stay as they are.

### Heading color (one source of truth)
The page CSP blocks scripts but allows inline style. `PreviewCoordinator` should prepend a `:root` block to the stylesheet. Generate it from the same four constants that define `SilkwebEditorHeading`, so the hex values are never typed twice:
```css
:root { --sw-heading: #2A6A86; }
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { :root { --sw-heading: #86BCD6; } }
@media (prefers-contrast: more) { :root { --sw-heading: #1F5570; } }
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) and (prefers-contrast: more) { :root { --sw-heading: #A6D3E6; } }
```
WebKit resolves these media queries from the web view's `effectiveAppearance` and the Increase Contrast setting. `preview.css` then uses `color: var(--sw-heading, -apple-system-label)`. Add a test that the emitted block matches the Swift constants.

### CSS (replaces the two heading lines and the `.sw-doc` padding)
```css
.sw-doc { padding: 16px 40px 80px; }              /* was 32px top */
.sw-doc > :first-child { margin-top: 0; }
h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6 { color: var(--sw-heading, -apple-system-label); font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.25; }
h1 { font-size: 2em;     margin: 1.25em 0 .5em; padding-bottom: .25em; border-bottom: 1px solid -apple-system-separator; }
h2 { font-size: 1.5em;   margin: 1.5em 0 .5em; }
h3 { font-size: 1.25em;  margin: 1.4em 0 .4em; }
h4 { font-size: 1.125em; margin: 1.4em 0 .4em; }
h5 { font-size: 1em;     margin: 1.5em 0 .25em; font-weight: 700; }
h6 { font-size: .9375em; margin: 1.5em 0 .25em; font-weight: 700; }
:is(h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6) + :is(h2,h3,h4,h5,h6) { margin-top: .35em; }
```
In pixels: 32 / 24 / 20 / 18 / 16 / 15. H5 and H6 sit at body size, bold and tinted, and are clearly smaller than H3. The H1 rule spans the full content box (640px at the max column width) with 8px between the text and the rule. When a heading directly follows another, the margins collapse to the larger one. So H1 → H6 gets the H1's 16px below the rule and nothing more, which keeps a sub-heading tight to its title.

```
GOOD                                BAD (today)
 ⎯ tab bar ⎯                         ⎯ tab bar ⎯
   Settling In          (32, teal)
   ────────────────────────────        (big empty band)
   Jamestown, PA        (15, bold)     Settling In          (gray)
   Body text…                          Jamestown, PA        (≈H3 size)
```

### Split vs Preview-only
Both modes use the same CSS. In Split, the narrower pane keeps 40px side padding, and the 16px top padding roughly lines up the first heading with the editor's first line (1.50 sets the editor top inset to 16). Preview-only centers the 720px column as before.

### Print
The full print rules belong to 1.23. Leave a one-line comment in `preview.css` saying 1.23 mirrors these heading rules, forces the light `--sw-heading` value and adds `break-after: avoid` on headings.

### Accessibility
Every heading color meets at least 6:1 contrast (see 1.47). Level is shown by size, weight and color together. The H1 rule is decorative. Semantic `h1`–`h6` tags and TOC anchors stay unchanged.

### Out of scope
- An H2 rule (orchestrator decision: H1 only)
- Body font, size or color (1.24)
- Editor and Outline typography
- Theme settings
